package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

internal object StoryLibraryBatch69 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_midnight_bakery_ledger",
            title = "The Midnight Bakery Ledger",
            tagline = "A missing recipe book reveals the people who kept a family business alive.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "Flour on the Doorstep", listOf("The bakery on Cedar Road opened before sunrise and closed long after the last bus. When Imani began helping there on weekends, she learned that its owner, Auntie Sade, kept a ledger of every loaf donated to neighbours who needed one.", "One Monday, the ledger vanished. Auntie Sade was more upset about the names and notes inside than the money records. The book held years of small promises, including who had delivered meals during floods and who had helped repair the ovens.", "Imani found a dusting of flour on the back step and a torn paper corner caught beneath a crate. The corner carried half a sentence: 'Return it when the bell rings.'")),
                StoryPart(2, "The Bell at Four", listOf("The old handbell was rung at four each afternoon to announce that the day’s unsold bread was ready to be shared. It had been silent since the ledger disappeared.", "Imani asked the delivery driver, Mr Musa, whether he had seen anyone near the back step. He remembered a person carrying a blue umbrella, although the sky had been clear.", "Auntie Sade said the umbrella belonged to the community centre. Imani and her cousin Tayo decided to visit, but first they copied the remaining donation notes so the information would not be lost again.")),
                StoryPart(3, "The Community Centre", listOf("The centre's caretaker recognised the umbrella immediately. It belonged to a volunteer named Feyi, who had borrowed it during a sunny day because the handle made a useful walking stick.", "Feyi had not taken the ledger, but she remembered seeing a young person carrying a thick notebook. The person had asked where the bakery stored its old photographs.", "The caretaker pointed to a notice about the centre's history exhibition. A photograph showed the bakery's first oven and a group of volunteers standing beside it. On the back was the same bell symbol that appeared in the torn paper.")),
                StoryPart(4, "The First Oven", listOf("Auntie Sade explained that the bell had belonged to her grandmother, who started the bakery with a borrowed oven. The first ledger recorded flour shared by several families when supplies were scarce.", "Imani searched the bakery storeroom for the original photograph and found an envelope of receipts. One receipt listed a repair paid for by the community centre, even though the bakery had always told the story as if the family had managed alone.", "The receipt changed the question. Perhaps the missing ledger had been taken by someone trying to restore a forgotten part of the bakery's history.")),
                StoryPart(5, "A Name in Pencil", listOf("The exhibition organiser, Mr Ade, checked the photograph archive. He found a pencilled name beneath the group picture: Nneka, the apprentice who had designed the first sharing system.", "Nneka's contribution had disappeared from the bakery's official story after she moved away. Mr Ade remembered that she had donated a collection of notebooks to the centre years earlier.", "The notebooks were stored in labelled boxes. One was missing, and the borrowing card showed that a student named Bisi had checked it out for a school project. Imani recognised the name from her class.")),
                StoryPart(6, "Bisi's Project", listOf("Bisi was surprised when Imani asked about the notebook. She had borrowed it to research local businesses, then discovered that its pages contained personal addresses and notes that should not be displayed publicly.", "She had taken the bakery ledger too, intending to compare the records, but realised she had no permission. Rather than return it anonymously, she had panicked and hidden it in the centre's locked display cabinet.", "Bisi apologised and showed them the cabinet. She had not read the private notes aloud or copied them. Imani told her the important thing now was to return the book safely and decide together what could be shared.")),
                StoryPart(7, "What Belongs in the Archive", listOf("Auntie Sade was relieved to have the ledger back, but she agreed that the old records deserved proper care. With Bisi and Mr Ade, she sorted the pages into public history, business records, and private notes.", "They asked permission from living contributors or their families before displaying personal stories. Some names were kept private, while other volunteers were happy to be credited.", "Imani discovered that Nneka had created the four-o'clock sharing bell and the system that let neighbours contribute flour without keeping score. The bakery's tradition had begun as a community project, not a family rule.")),
                StoryPart(8, "The Recipe for a New Chapter", listOf("Bisi proposed an exhibition panel explaining how the bakery and community centre had supported each other. Auntie Sade added a recipe card for the first shared loaf, though she warned that the measurements were written for an enormous oven.", "Imani and Tayo tested a smaller version with the baker. Their first attempt was too dense, the second too dry, and the third finally came out soft enough to share.", "They wrote the corrected measurements in a new recipe book and credited Nneka's original notes. The new book would be kept in the bakery, while the old ledger stayed safely in the archive.")),
                StoryPart(9, "The Four-O'clock Gathering", listOf("On exhibition day, the handbell rang at four. Neighbours arrived with photographs, recipes, and memories of the bakery's early years.", "Bisi explained why some pages were not displayed. People appreciated that the archive protected private information instead of treating every old note as public property.", "Auntie Sade invited Nneka's family to add their own account. They brought a photograph of Nneka teaching apprentices and a letter describing how proud she had been of the sharing bell.")),
                StoryPart(10, "A Ledger of Thanks", listOf("The bakery created a new ledger for donations, with clear rules about what information would be recorded and who could read it. The historical ledger was preserved by the community centre, and the exhibition told the story of everyone who had helped.", "Imani added one final entry to the new book: 'Today, the bell rang because the neighbours came together.' Auntie Sade signed beneath it, followed by Bisi, Tayo, and Mr Ade.", "The bakery still opened before sunrise, but its history had become larger and more honest. Imani understood that a tradition was not made by one person alone. It lasted because people cared for it, corrected it, and made room for others to belong.")
                )
            )
        )
    )
}
